<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<!--
  The Android host application.

  docs/security/PROVISIONING.md section 8 forbids requesting a permission the
  flow does not use. INTERNET is the sync flow's: the address the user enters
  in settings is an https endpoint, and neither the pull of
  `SYNC_PROTOCOL_V1.md` section 7 nor its bootstrap of section 6 can run
  without the network. It is a normal permission, so no prompt and no runtime
  gate exist to satisfy. Import needs none: the photo picker and the document
  picker return a content URI the application already has permission to read.

  The media read permissions belong to one step, the source deletion of
  DESIGN.md section 15.3 and ANDROID.md section 14.5. The owner chose them over
  only telling the user where to delete: a picked item is a grant to read that
  one item, so Chur needs read access to find the original in the library before
  it has the system ask the user and delete it. They are requested at that step,
  after the user chooses "Review source deletion", and never before; "Keep
  original" asks for nothing, and a refusal leaves the import as it was.
  READ_MEDIA_VISUAL_USER_SELECTED lets the user grant only chosen photos and
  videos on API 34 and later, and READ_EXTERNAL_STORAGE is the same read access
  on API 30 to 32. API 29 has no system delete request, so there the step asks
  for nothing; READ_EXTERNAL_STORAGE is declared on API 29 too, because a
  permission entry cannot set a lowest API level, but nothing requests it there.

  ACCESS_LOCAL_NETWORK belongs to sync with a server on the user's own
  network, ANDROID.md section 24. From Android 17, the API level this app
  targets, a connection on the local link (Wi-Fi or Ethernet, never a VPN)
  to a private, shared (100.64.0.0/10), link-local, or unique local address,
  to an IPv6 address on a directly connected route, or to a `.local` name
  needs it, and without it the connection usually just times out. The owner
  chose to support such servers rather than refuse them. It is requested when
  the user connects to or syncs with a server whose address is local, and
  never at launch or by the background worker; a refusal leaves sync as it
  was, and Settings says how to allow it. It is a runtime permission of the
  Nearby devices group, unknown to API 36 and lower.

  ACCESS_NETWORK_STATE serves the same step, and WorkManager declares it as
  well. It is a normal permission, with no prompt. It tells whether the active
  network is a VPN, which Android does not guard, so a server the user reaches
  through their VPN brings no request, and which IPv6 routes are on the link.
  Nothing else Chur does touches a permission-guarded API.

  Backup is on and governed by rule files, per ANDROID.md section 13.4 and the
  "Public-shell disclosure and backup" section of docs/product/DISCREET_MODE.md.
  The two halves are deliberate and disjoint: public-shell storage is included,
  because a Notes surface that loses its content on device transfer is not
  functional and a shell nobody uses announces what it hides; vault storage is
  excluded, because an object store carried by a transport that chooses what to
  copy is not restorable, and BACKUP_FORMAT_V1.md is how vault content leaves
  the device.

  An <include> set makes everything else excluded, so the rules fail closed
  rather than needing to name the vault as the storage layout changes, which is
  what opting out of backup entirely used to buy. scripts/check-backup-rules.py
  fails the build if either file ever names a vault path. The sync state file
  is not named anywhere in those rules, and that is what keeps it excluded: it
  holds the device's transport token, which section 7 of the sync protocol
  puts in storage that never leaves the device.
-->
<manifest xmlns:android="http://schemas.android.com/apk/res/android">

    <uses-permission android:name="android.permission.INTERNET" />
    <uses-permission android:name="android.permission.ACCESS_LOCAL_NETWORK" />
    <uses-permission android:name="android.permission.ACCESS_NETWORK_STATE" />
    <uses-permission android:name="android.permission.READ_MEDIA_IMAGES" />
    <uses-permission android:name="android.permission.READ_MEDIA_VIDEO" />
    <uses-permission android:name="android.permission.READ_MEDIA_AUDIO" />
    <uses-permission android:name="android.permission.READ_MEDIA_VISUAL_USER_SELECTED" />
    <uses-permission
        android:name="android.permission.READ_EXTERNAL_STORAGE"
        android:maxSdkVersion="32" />

    <application
        android:allowBackup="true"
        android:dataExtractionRules="@xml/data_extraction_rules"
        android:fullBackupContent="@xml/backup_rules"
        android:hasFragileUserData="false"
        android:icon="@mipmap/ic_launcher"
        android:label="@string/app_name"
        android:supportsRtl="true"
        android:theme="@style/Theme.Chur">

        <activity
            android:name=".MainActivity"
            android:configChanges="orientation|screenSize|screenLayout|keyboardHidden|uiMode"
            android:exported="true"
            android:label="@string/app_name"
            android:launchMode="singleTask"
            android:windowSoftInputMode="adjustResize">
            <intent-filter>
                <action android:name="android.intent.action.MAIN" />
                <category android:name="android.intent.category.LAUNCHER" />
            </intent-filter>
        </activity>
        <provider
            android:name="androidx.core.content.FileProvider"
            android:authorities="${applicationId}.exports"
            android:exported="false"
            android:grantUriPermissions="true">
            <meta-data
                android:name="android.support.FILE_PROVIDER_PATHS"
                android:resource="@xml/export_paths" />
        </provider>
        <provider
            android:name=".DeviceImportProvider"
            android:authorities="${applicationId}.device-import"
            android:exported="false" />
    </application>
</manifest>
